\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1 Auxiliary attention losses converge under teacher forcing (GAR\,=\,0.50) but collapse by an order of magnitude under autoregressive generation on two architectures. A five-way diagnostic comparison identifies exposure bias as a primary contributing factor; $\beta$-steering recovers GAR above the TF baseline, confirming attention misallocation as a proximal cause (KV cache drift remains untested). Scheduled sampling compresses the gap to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (Qwen) and ${\sim}$2.9$\times$ (Llama), generalizing across architectures and seeds---the first such transfer to auxiliary attention objectives in multi-turn transformers.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. TF evaluation uses IID UltraChat and 50 held-out conversations; AR evaluation uses $N$\,=\,50 held-out conversations (mean 3.6 user turns). All fine-tuned variants converge under TF ($\leq$2\,pp spread); the 8--17$\times$ TF-AR gap persists across all configurations. $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$: learned gate mean and std across 28 layers (LoRA-only has no gates). GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc cc c}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID (TF)}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out (TF)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{AR ($N$\,=\,50)}} & \\
