\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA \citep{hu2022lora,dettmers2023qlora} on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat \citep{ding2023ultrachat} training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB; training costs in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-cost}). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; dataset statistics in Appendices~\ref{sec:appendix-data} and~\ref{sec:appendix-dataset-ext}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and perplexity (PPL).

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge: GAR-ours spans 0.49--0.51 ($\leq$2\,pp), PPL is 2.78 for all. IID and held-out protocols produce identical rankings (held-out GAR-ours differs by $\leq$1\,pp), ruling out data overlap as a confound. GAR-Dongre remains low (0.01--0.02) with no TF-AR gap, confirming that only concentrated last-token attention is vulnerable to distribution shift; scheduled sampling breaks this pattern (\S\ref{subsec:ss}).

Under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}). The ordering (ThreadGate 0.064 $>$ LoRA-only 0.037 $>$ Uniform Gate 0.033 $>$ PCGrad 0.030 $>$ Base 0.019) shows ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only is significant ($p$\,=\,0.004; Appendices~\ref{sec:appendix-stats} and~\ref{sec:appendix-stats-ext}), yet the gap remains 7.8$\times$. A second seed confirms TF stability ($0.504\pm0.001$). MMLU 5-shot accuracy remains within 0.8\,pp across all variants (per-category breakdown in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-mmlu-ext}). We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation as a proximal cause (9\% PPL cost at $\beta$\,=\,3; phase transition at $\beta \in [2, 3]$).}
  \label{fig:steering-recovery}
\end{figure}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. If the collapse is caused by attention misallocation rather than representation degradation, then directly correcting attention should recover GAR. We test this by adding a constant bias $\beta$ to pre-softmax attention logits for goal-token positions during generation:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{A}_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} = A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} + \beta \cdot \mathbf{1}[j \in \mathcal{G}],
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{A}$ denotes the modified logits before softmax. The additive form operates in log-probability space, providing uniform boost even when baseline attention is near zero. The response is sharply nonlinear: $\beta \in [0, 2]$ yields marginal improvement, then $\beta = 3$ triggers a phase transition to GAR\,=\,0.556 (9\% PPL cost). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77); the operating region $\beta \in [3, 5]$ exceeds the TF baseline while PPL remains below 1.8 (Figure~\ref{fig:steering-recovery}; full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}; per-$\beta$ turn-level trajectories in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering-viz}). GAR recovery under steering is partly by construction: boosting attention logits toward goal tokens mechanically increases the attention-based metric. The stronger evidence is the counterfactual: if the collapse were due to hidden-state drift rather than attention misallocation, reweighting could not recover GAR, yet it does---consistent with attention misallocation as a proximal cause (qualitative examples in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-examples}). Downstream, steering ($\beta$\,=\,3) raises late-turn instruction compliance by +11.1\,pp, though this effect does not reach conventional significance ($p$\,=\,0.077; content-constrained tasks benefit most, +23.8\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ir}).

\subsection{Scheduled Sampling Compresses the TF--AR Gap}\label{subsec:ss}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_gap_compression.pdf}
  \caption{Scheduled sampling compresses the TF-AR gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$, the only variant approaching parity across five training conditions.}
  \label{fig:ss-gap}
\end{figure}

Scheduled sampling (SS; \citealp{bengio2015scheduled}), which mixes model-generated tokens into training prefixes with linearly increasing probability (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ss}), directly targets the training-inference mismatch. Across three seeds, AR GAR-ours rises to $0.401\pm0.047$, compressing the gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (TF GAR $0.498\pm0.004$) with no PPL cost ($1.441\pm0.001$; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-gap}; per-seed details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-perseed}). Steering on SS models nearly eliminates seed variance ($\beta$\,=\,5: $0.717\pm0.001$, std 0.047$\to$0.001; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-multiseed}). SS generalizes across GAR definitions (mid-turn GAR above 0.36 for all token-position variants; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gar-robust}; extended robustness in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gar-ext}) and across architectures: on Llama-3.1-8B, SS compresses the gap from ${\sim}$27$\times$ to 2.9$\times$, weaker than Qwen likely due to fewer training epochs (2 vs.\ 3; \S\ref{subsec:cross-model}). The benefit diminishes in longer dialogues (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), consistent with exposure bias compounding over turns.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is central to how large language models are deployed, yet a persistent failure accompanies even the most fluent generation: as conversations grow longer, models pay progressively less attention to the system-prompt goals that define their task. \citet{dongre2026attention} measured 27--48\% Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) decline across 12 models over 50 turns, even as residual stream probes confirm that goal representations persist (AUC\,=\,0.98). This is not just a measurement artifact---exploratory evaluation suggests it can degrade instruction compliance by up to 24 percentage points on content-constrained tasks (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Existing remedies address this decay at inference time. Split-softmax \citep{li2024measuring} separates instruction from conversation tokens; StreamingLLM \citep{xiao2024streaming} retains anchor tokens; Gated Attention \citep{qiu2025gated} applies per-head sigmoid gates within a single forward pass. None of these intervenes during training. A natural alternative is to optimize goal attention directly through auxiliary attention losses trained alongside next-token prediction (NTP). Yet this strategy rests on an untested assumption: that attention patterns learned under teacher forcing transfer to autoregressive generation. Classical exposure bias research \citep{bengio2015scheduled,lamb2016professor} has shown that teacher forcing creates a training--inference mismatch for RNN sequence models, but whether this mismatch extends to auxiliary attention objectives in multi-turn transformers remains unknown.

We test this assumption. We introduce ThreadGate, per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned gates on the residual stream ($x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$), designed not as a solution but as a controlled testbed for isolating failure mechanisms. A five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) forms a causal exclusion chain (\S\ref{sec:comparison-design}): each adjacent pair rules out one alternative hypothesis---turn conditioning, gating mechanism, gradient direction conflict---tracing the failure to its primary contributing factor.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_headline.pdf}
  \caption{Exposure bias collapses auxiliary attention losses. \textbf{(a)}~Goal attention decays 85--90\% across turns under AR generation on both Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B. \textbf{(b)}~Under TF evaluation all variants reach GAR$\approx$0.50, but AR generation collapses GAR by 8--17$\times$ (log scale). \textbf{(c)}~Scheduled sampling compresses the TF--AR gap from 7.8$\times$ to 1.3$\times$ (Qwen) and ${\sim}$27$\times$ to 2.9$\times$ (Llama).}
  \label{fig:headline}
\end{figure*}

Training succeeds but inference fails. All variants converge to TF GAR\,=\,0.50, yet under AR generation GAR collapses by an order of magnitude on both architectures (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}). The causal exclusion identifies exposure bias as the primary contributing factor: PCGrad removes gradient direction conflict yet \emph{worsens} AR GAR ($p$\,=\,0.002); gating provides no benefit over LoRA-only. Steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation; scheduled sampling compresses the gap to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (Qwen) and ${\sim}$2.9$\times$ (Llama) with no perplexity cost.

Our contributions: (1)~the first empirical characterization of exposure bias on auxiliary attention losses (order-of-magnitude gap across two architectures and scales); (2)~a five-way diagnostic testbed informed by seven loss iterations, enabling causal exclusion of gradient direction conflict via PCGrad ($p$\,=\,0.002, effect reverses); (3)~$\beta$-steering confirms attention misallocation as a proximal cause; (4)~scheduled sampling compresses the gap to 1.3--2.9$\times$, the first transfer to auxiliary attention objectives in multi-turn transformers ($0.401\pm0.047$, three seeds).

